Subject: [PATCH 7/7] Common: do not call emscripten_sleep() in a pthreads build

sleep_ms(), sleep_us() and sleep_precise() all take an __EMSCRIPTEN__ branch
that calls emscripten_sleep(). That is an ASYNCIFY primitive: in a build
without -sASYNCIFY it does not sleep, it aborts the calling thread with

    Aborted(Please compile your program with async support in order to use
    asynchronous operations like emscripten_sleep)

which is what a browser build hits within a second of starting, on whichever
worker sleeps first.

The branch reads like a leftover from the asm.js era, when Emscripten had no
threads and blocking anywhere was impossible. With -pthread the threads that
sleep here are workers, and a worker can block for real — so a threaded build
takes the POSIX path like every other threaded platform. The single-threaded
branch is left as it was, guarded by __EMSCRIPTEN_PTHREADS__.

diff --git a/Common/TimeUtil.cpp b/Common/TimeUtil.cpp
index e01c3108e..2d01bb7cb 100644
--- a/Common/TimeUtil.cpp
+++ b/Common/TimeUtil.cpp
@@ -305,7 +305,12 @@ void sleep_ms(int ms, const char *reason) {
 	Sleep(ms);
 #elif defined(HAVE_LIBNX)
 	svcSleepThread(ms * 1000000);
-#elif defined(__EMSCRIPTEN__)
+#elif defined(__EMSCRIPTEN__) && !defined(__EMSCRIPTEN_PTHREADS__)
+	// emscripten_sleep() is an ASYNCIFY primitive: without -sASYNCIFY it does not sleep,
+	// it aborts the calling thread at runtime. That is survivable only for a
+	// single-threaded build, which is what this branch is left for. A build with
+	// -pthread sleeps on a worker, and a worker can block for real, so it takes the
+	// POSIX path below like every other threaded platform.
 	emscripten_sleep(ms);
 #else
 	usleep(ms * 1000);
@@ -323,8 +328,8 @@ void sleep_us(int us, const char *reason) {
 	Sleep(us / 1000);
 #elif defined(HAVE_LIBNX)
 	svcSleepThread(us * 1000);
-#elif defined(__EMSCRIPTEN__)
-	emscripten_sleep(us / 1000);
+#elif defined(__EMSCRIPTEN__) && !defined(__EMSCRIPTEN_PTHREADS__)
+	emscripten_sleep(us / 1000);  // See sleep_ms().
 #else
 	usleep(us);
 #endif
@@ -380,8 +385,8 @@ void sleep_precise(double seconds, const char *reason) {
 	// On other platforms, we just do a conversion with more input precision than in sleep_ms which is restricted to whole milliseconds.
 #elif defined(HAVE_LIBNX)
 	svcSleepThread((int64_t)(seconds * 1000000000.0));
-#elif defined(__EMSCRIPTEN__)
-	emscripten_sleep(seconds * 1000.0);
+#elif defined(__EMSCRIPTEN__) && !defined(__EMSCRIPTEN_PTHREADS__)
+	emscripten_sleep(seconds * 1000.0);  // See sleep_ms().
 #else
 	usleep(seconds * 1000000.0);
 #endif
